An escape lane with a byte that is not UTF-8 (`bad\xff.example`, between two
good entries). The shell's list_json keeps the line — `jq -R` turns the byte
into U+FFFD — provided its grep/sed run under the C locale (in a UTF-8 shell
BSD sed stopped at the byte and every entry after it was lost); rowt-rs read
such a file as EMPTY (read_to_string failed and the error became ""), which
emptied the whole lane and, with the lane rules as live rule-sets, reached the
running router on the next render. Both now render good.example, bad\uFFFD.example
and last.example into escape-2.json, byte for byte.

Not compared, and different: `escape list`/`dump` print the raw byte on the
shell side and U+FFFD on the Rust side, and a Rust-side edit (`add`, `rm`)
writes the file back with U+FFFD in place of the byte, where the shell's
append keeps it. Recorded in PORTING.md's 6.7 table.
